[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
BGP in the fabric is healthy; there is no evidence of session flapping. On every router (both leaves, both spines, and the super-spine) all BGP neighbors are Established, with “Connections established 1; dropped 0” and no resets recorded. Example: leaf_router_0_0 shows peers 172.16.0.4 (spine_router_0_0) and 172.16.0.8 (spine_router_0_1) Established; leaf_router_0_1 shows the same toward its spines; spines toward super_spine_router_0 are likewise steady. FRR services are active (running) across all nodes.

End-to-end reachability between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is solid: targeted tests with 20 rapid pings in both directions had 0% loss with ~1 ms RTT. Routes on the leaves are correct (e.g., leaf_router_0_0 has 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4; leaf_router_0_1 has 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6), and interface statistics show no errors/drops.

Mechanism: If BGP adjacencies were bouncing, we would see neighbor drops, reduced “Up/Down” timers, or missing remote /24 routes and intermittent ping loss. None of these are present. Localization: no fabric device or interface exhibits instability. Root cause: no BGP fault found; the reported intermittent reachability is not reproducible and was likely transient outside the fabric (e.g., short-lived host ARP/cache event).

Corrective fix: No device restart or neighbor clear is warranted. If symptoms reappear, capture host ARP/ICMP during the event and check interface flaps/logs, but do not restart any FRR process based on current evidence.
=== END ===